Issues.Body:
### Description

Since #62162 (11.0 preview 4), `DefaultHubDispatcher.StartActivity` adds connection endpoint tags, which reads `IConnectionEndPointFeature.LocalEndPoint` from the connection's features. It does so even with no listener, since `Critical` logging is on by default.

`HttpConnectionDispatcher` sets that feature from each request: `connection.Features.Set(context.Features.Get<IConnectionEndPointFeature>())`. On IIS in-process the feature is the `IISHttpContext`, which reads the local endpoint lazily from the native `HTTP_REQUEST`. With long polling, that request has completed by the time the next hub invocation or `OnDisconnected` runs, so `NativeRequestContext.GetEndPoint` reads memory that IIS has already released.

On our server this took down the w3wp of a Blazor Server app twice in one day:

```
Application: w3wp.exe
CoreCLR Version: 11.0.26.50505
.NET Version: 11.0.0-rc.2.26504.105
Description: The process was terminated due to an unhandled exception.
Exception Info: System.AccessViolationException: Attempted to read or write protected memory. This is often an indication that other memory is corrupt.
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.HttpSys.Internal.SocketAddress.CopyOutAddress(Windows.Win32.Networking.WinSock.SOCKADDR*)
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.HttpSys.Internal.NativeRequestContext.GetEndPoint(Boolean)
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.IIS.Core.IISHttpContext.InitializeLocalEndpoint()
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.IIS.Core.IISHttpContext.Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features.IHttpConnectionFeature.get_LocalIpAddress()
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.IIS.Core.IISHttpContext.Microsoft.AspNetCore.Connections.Features.IConnectionEndPointFeature.get_LocalEndPoint()
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Shared.ConnectionEndpointTags.AddConnectionEndpointTags(System.Diagnostics.TagList ByRef, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features.IFeatureCollection)
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Internal.DefaultHubDispatcher`1.StartActivity(...)
```

### Repro

11.0.0-rc.2.26504.105, published and hosted in-process. IIS Express is enough.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Connections.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Connections;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddSignalR(o => o.AddFilter<ProbeFilter>());
var app = builder.Build();

app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    var feature = context.Features.Get<IConnectionEndPointFeature>();
    var request = $"{context.Request.Method} {context.Request.Path}{context.Request.QueryString}";
    context.Response.OnCompleted(() =>
    {
        if (feature is not null) Probe.Completed.AddOrUpdate(feature, request);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    });
    await next();
});

app.MapHub<EchoHub>("/hub", o => o.Transports = HttpTransportType.LongPolling);
app.MapGet("/probe", () => string.Join('\n', Probe.Log));
app.Run();

static class Probe
{
    public static readonly ConditionalWeakTable<IConnectionEndPointFeature, string> Completed = new();
    public static readonly ConcurrentQueue<string> Log = new();
}

class EchoHub : Hub
{
    public string Echo(string s) => s;
}

class ProbeFilter : IHubFilter
{
    public ValueTask<object?> InvokeMethodAsync(HubInvocationContext invocationContext, Func<HubInvocationContext, ValueTask<object?>> next)
    {
        var feature = invocationContext.Context.Features.Get<IConnectionEndPointFeature>();
        var done = feature is not null && Probe.Completed.TryGetValue(feature, out var request) ? $"a request that already completed: {request}" : "a live request";
        Probe.Log.Enqueue($"{invocationContext.HubMethodName}: endpoint feature {feature?.GetType().Name} belongs to {done}; LocalEndPoint read by the tag code = {feature?.LocalEndPoint}");
        return next(invocationContext);
    }
}
```

Then, with any HTTP client:

1. `POST /hub/negotiate?negotiateVersion=1` and take the connection token
2. `GET /hub?id=<token>` (first poll)
3. `POST /hub?id=<token>` with `{"protocol":"json","version":1}\x1e`
4. `GET /hub?id=<token>` (second poll, returns the handshake response)
5. Without polling again, `POST /hub?id=<token>` with `{"type":1,"invocationId":"1","target":"Echo","arguments":["x"]}\x1e`

`GET /probe` then shows, for every run:

```
Echo: endpoint feature IISHttpContextOfT`1 belongs to a request that already completed: GET /hub?id=...; LocalEndPoint read by the tag code = [::1]:5099
```

Most reads still return the right address because IIS hasn't reused that memory yet. When it has, the process crashes as above. Doing nothing after step 4 until the connection times out does the same read from `OnDisconnected`.

### Expected behavior

The hub never reads a completed request. For example, take the endpoints when they are copied to the connection, the way `CloneHttpContext` already does for `LocalIpAddress` and `LocalPort`.

### Notes

.NET 10 is not affected: its `StartActivity` doesn't read the connection's endpoint, and its IIS server has no `IConnectionEndPointFeature`. Reading `HttpContext.Connection.LocalIpAddress` in a middleware while the request is alive should avoid it, since `IISHttpContext` caches the address once read.

### Is there an existing issue for this?

- [x] I have searched the existing issues

### Describe the bug

Blazor Hybrid hosts can't be published with `PublishAot=true`. MAUI, WinForms and WPF are tied to frameworks that aren't AOT-capable, but third-party hosts on AOT-friendly WebView bindings can be. But `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebView` breaks at runtime under NativeAOT.

The WebView assembly itself produces these warnings on publish (10.0.13):

```
IL2026/IL3050  IpcCommon.Serialize(String,Object[]): JsonSerializer.Serialize<IEnumerable<Object>>(..., JsonSerializerOptions)
IL2026/IL3050  IpcCommon.TryDeserialize<T>(...): JsonSerializer.Deserialize(ReadOnlySpan<Char>, Type, JsonSerializerOptions)
IL2067         WebViewRenderer.AddRootComponent(Type,String): 'componentType' does not satisfy 'DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.All'
IL3000         WebViewManager.StaticWebAssetsLoader.ResolveRelativeToAssembly(): Assembly.Location
```

The assembly is also not marked `IsTrimmable`, so with `TrimMode=partial` it is rooted entirely.

These map to three runtime failures and one cleanup item:

You're right. I changed the content instead of just formatting it. Here's the original text with only Markdown formatting applied.

1. **Incoming IPC messages can't be parsed.** `IpcCommon.TryDeserialize` deserializes `JsonElement[]` through `JsonSerializerOptionsProvider.Options`.
   - With the AOT default (`JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=false`), those options have no resolver, so the very first message (`AttachPage`) throws `InvalidOperationException: Reflection-based serialization has been disabled`.
   - With reflection re-enabled, it throws `NotSupportedException: ArrayConverter<JsonElement[], JsonElement> is missing native code`, because the value-type generic instantiation is never compiled.
2. **Outgoing IPC messages with value-type arguments can't be serialized.** `IpcCommon.Serialize` serializes an `IEnumerable<object>` reflectively, so argument types are only known at runtime:
   - `BeginInvokeJS`: `JSCallResultType` / `JSCallType` enums → `EnumConverter<JSCallResultType> is missing native code`, so **every JS interop call fails**.
   - `Navigate`: the `NavigationOptions` struct → `ObjectDefaultConverter<NavigationOptions> is missing native code`, so **`NavigationManager.NavigateTo` fails** (logged by `WebViewNavigationManager`, not thrown).
   - `int`/`long`/`string`/`bool`/`byte[]` arguments only work when reflection is enabled; with the AOT default nothing can be serialized at all.
3. **Root components lose their constructors.** `WebViewManager.AddRootComponentAsync(Type componentType, ...)`, the private `RootComponent.ComponentType` that stores it, and `WebViewRenderer.AddRootComponent(Type, string)` have no `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers(Component)]`. Nothing tells the trimmer to keep the component, so attaching the page throws `InvalidOperationException: A suitable constructor for type '...' could not be located`. This also affects plain trimming (`PublishTrimmed`), not only AOT.
4. **(Cleanup) `StaticWebAssetsLoader.ResolveRelativeToAssembly` uses `Assembly.Location`.** It already returns `null` when `Location` is empty, and the dev-time manifest doesn't exist in published apps, so it's harmless at runtime. `AppContext.BaseDirectory` plus the entry assembly name would remove the warning.

### Expected Behavior

`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebView` publishes without trim/AOT warnings from its own code and works under NativeAOT with the default `JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=false`. The IPC payloads are a closed set of internal types, so source-generated System.Text.Json metadata can cover them. Reflection-based paths in `Microsoft.JSInterop` and `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web` (user-defined interop types, event args) are a separate concern and out of scope here.

### Steps To Reproduce

A headless console app, with no WebView2 or UI. A minimal `WebViewManager` subclass plays the role of `blazor.webview.js` and drives each IPC path through public APIs:
1. Receive `AttachPage`.
2. Observe `AttachToDocument` / `RenderBatch`.
3. Acknowledge the render batch, so the component's `OnAfterRenderAsync` calls `IJSRuntime.InvokeVoidAsync` (`BeginInvokeJS`).
4. Call `NavigationManager.NavigateTo` (`Navigate`).

Results on Windows x64, .NET SDK 10.0.402, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebView 10.0.13:

| Step | JIT | AOT (defaults) | AOT + reflection JSON | AOT + reflection JSON + `JsonElement[]` rooted | ... + app assembly rooted |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Receive `AttachPage` | PASS | FAIL: reflection disabled | FAIL: `ArrayConverter<JsonElement[],JsonElement>` missing | FAIL: `Probe` constructor trimmed | PASS |
| Send `AttachToDocument` / `RenderBatch` | PASS | FAIL | FAIL | FAIL | PASS |
| Send `BeginInvokeJS` | PASS | FAIL | FAIL | FAIL | FAIL: `EnumConverter<JSCallResultType>` missing |
| Send `Navigate` | PASS | FAIL | FAIL | FAIL: `ObjectDefaultConverter<NavigationOptions>` missing | FAIL: same |

The last two columns use workarounds (an rd.xml rooting the converter, and `TrimmerRootAssembly` for the app) only to get past each failure and reveal the next one.

```
dotnet run -c Release -p:PublishAot=false                                     # JIT
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 -o out\1                                 # AOT defaults
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 -o out\2 -p:JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=true
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 -o out\3 -p:JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=true -p:RootJsonElementArray=true
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 -o out\4 -p:JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=true -p:RootJsonElementArray=true -p:RootApp=true
```

