.

### Is there an existing issue for this?

- [x] I have searched the existing issues

Closest I found is #69596 (same area, `CircuitManager.reconnect`/`startConnection`), but there the connection fails to _start_. Here the connection starts fine and the hub invocation after it fails.

### Describe the bug

If an automatic reconnection attempt opens the SignalR connection but the `ConnectCircuit` invocation then throws (a hub error, a server timeout, a dropped response), the connection is left in the `Connected` state. The reconnection loop catches the exception and schedules another attempt. That next attempt calls `CircuitManager.reconnect()`, which returns `true` right away because the connection is already connected:

```ts
// src/Components/Web.JS/src/Platform/Circuits/CircuitManager.ts (main)
public async reconnect(): Promise<boolean> {
  ...
  if (this._connection!.state === HubConnectionState.Connected) {
    return true;            // <- no ConnectCircuit, no onConnectionUp()
  }
  this._connection = await this.startConnection();
  ...
  if (!await this._connection!.invoke<boolean>('ConnectCircuit', this._circuitId)) { ... }
  this._options.reconnectionHandler!.onConnectionUp();
  return true;
}
```

`ReconnectionProcess.attemptPeriodicReconnection` treats `true` as success and returns, but nothing hides the reconnect display, because `onConnectionUp()` was never called. The loop has ended, `failed()` is never called, and the circuit is not connected. The user sees "Reconnecting..." indefinitely, with no Retry button and no further attempts. `_currentReconnectionProcess` is only cleared in `onConnectionUp()`, so a later `onclose` doesn't start a new process either.

```
attempt k:   startConnection() ✅  → invoke('ConnectCircuit') throws → caught, loop continues
attempt k+1: reconnect(): state === Connected → return true
             loop: result === true → return            (no hide, no failed)
UI:          "Reconnecting..." forever; Blazor.reconnect() from a custom Retry also returns true
```

The same happens through `resume()`. After a refused `ConnectCircuit` (`false`), the loop calls `resumeCallback()`. Because `onclose` always calls `_pausingState.transitionTo(true)`, `resume()` passes its "not paused" check and invokes `ResumeCircuit` on the still-open connection. If that invocation throws, the exception reaches the loop's `catch`, and the next attempt hits the same `return true` above.

### Expected Behavior

A reconnection attempt whose `ConnectCircuit`/`ResumeCircuit` invocation fails should not leave a connected-but-circuitless connection behind. The next attempt should either really reconnect the circuit, or the process should end in `failed()`/`rejected()`, so the user gets a Retry or Reload.

Possible fixes, either would do:

- stop the connection when the `ConnectCircuit` (or `ResumeCircuit`) invocation throws, before rethrowing; or
- in `reconnect()`, only short-circuit on `Connected` when the circuit is known to be connected (e.g. track a "circuit connected" flag set where `onConnectionUp()` is called), otherwise invoke `ConnectCircuit` on the existing connection.

### Steps To Reproduce

Minimal repro: https://github.com/eeroglu/blazor-reconnect-connectcircuit-repro

It is `dotnet new blazor --interactivity Server --empty` plus one script in `Components/App.razor`. The script wraps the connection in `configureSignalR` so the next `ConnectCircuit` invocation rejects, as a hub error or server timeout would, and a button closes the connection the way a network drop does.

1. `dotnet run`, open the app, click **Increment** once.
2. Click **Drop the connection; the next ConnectCircuit fails**.
3. Attempt 1 opens a new connection and its `ConnectCircuit` rejects. Attempt 2 returns `true` at once. The dialog stays on "Rejoining the server..." (checked at 3 s, 15 s and 45 s) with `connection.state === 'Connected'` and no further attempts.

Control: `connection.stop()` alone (same drop, no rejection) closes the dialog within a second, and the counter works.

### Exceptions (if any)

Only the one from the failed attempt, logged by `ReconnectionProcess` at Error level. Nothing after that.

### .NET Version

SDK 10.0.112, Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 10.0.12. The same code is in 10.0.13 and 11.0.0-rc.2 (checked the shipped `blazor.web.js`) and on `main` (f73a8df896).

### Anything else?

We work around it by closing the connection when either invocation rejects, via `configureSignalR`:

```js
connection.invoke = function (methodName) {
  const call = invoke.apply(connection, arguments);
  if (methodName !== "ConnectCircuit" && methodName !== "ResumeCircuit")
    return call;
  return call.catch((error) => {
    const rethrow = () => {
      throw error;
    };
    // Await the close: on Long Polling a late onclose would undo the next attempt's reconnect.
    return connection.stop().then(rethrow, rethrow);
  });
};
```

That relies on the internal hub method names, so a framework fix would be much better.

- OS: macOS (osx-arm64), Chromium (Playwright 1.58). Not browser-specific: the logic is in `CircuitManager`/`ReconnectionProcess`.

## Background and Motivation

The `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` package provides typed projections of browser APIs. This proposal adds browser URL construction, parsing, mutation, serialization, query parameter manipulation, and live query parameter iteration.

The originating feature request is [dotnet/aspnetcore#69881](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/69881). The implementation is [MayaKirova/aspnetcore@0d21bfdb207678e4780d6ce12c6a49fafc840cfa](https://github.com/MayaKirova/aspnetcore/commit/0d21bfdb207678e4780d6ce12c6a49fafc840cfa) on the `mkirova/url-api` branch.

The URL and URLSearchParams constructor facades are reached from `IBrowserPlatform.Window`. A `Url` returned by construction or parsing is caller-owned and implements `IAsyncDisposable`. Its `SearchParams` property is a stable borrowed wrapper owned by the URL. A standalone URLSearchParams object instead has a `UrlSearchParamsOwner`, allowing the same non-disposable `UrlSearchParams` API to represent both borrowed and caller-created objects.

Web IDL iteration is projected as `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`. Each enumeration acquires a fresh native iterator on its first advance and reads it live rather than taking a snapshot. `await foreach` disposes the enumerator on completion, early exit, or an exception. The collection's owner must remain alive until enumeration ends; disposing it causes subsequent advances to throw `ObjectDisposedException`. A token supplied through `WithCancellation` is observed between browser operations, without aborting an in-flight operation or changing the default interop timeout.

This is an explicit iteration-protocol mapping decision: `entries()`, `keys()`, and `values()` return async sequences rather than `ValueTask`-wrapped public iterator objects. The underlying browser iterator is synchronous; advancing it is asynchronous across the browser interop boundary.

The linked commit contains the original explicit iterator design. The async-enumeration revision is currently a local change on `mkirova/url-api` and is not represented by that commit.

Object URL operations are deferred to a separate File API slice because `URL.createObjectURL()` requires a projected `Blob` or `MediaSource`. The callback-based `URLSearchParams.forEach()` operation is also deferred.

## Usage Examples

Register and inject the browser platform:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddBrowserPlatform();
```

```razor
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform
@inject IBrowserPlatform Browser
```

Construct, mutate, and serialize a URL:

```csharp
await using var url = await Browser.Window.Url.CreateAsync(
    "../products",
    "https://example.test/catalog/");

await url.SetSearchAsync("?tag=one");
await url.SearchParams.AppendAsync("tag", "two");
await url.SetHashAsync("#details");

var href = await url.GetHrefAsync();
// https://example.test/products?tag=one&tag=two#details
```

Parse without throwing for an invalid URL:

```csharp
await using var parsed = await Browser.Window.Url.ParseAsync(
    "/products",
    "https://example.test/");

if (parsed is not null)
{
    var origin = await parsed.GetOriginAsync();
}
```

Create standalone query parameters from an ordered sequence, retaining duplicate names:

```csharp
IEnumerable<KeyValuePair<string, string>> values =
[
    new("tag", "one"),
    new("tag", "two"),
];

await using var owner = await Browser.Window.UrlSearchParams.CreateAsync(values);
var searchParams = owner.Value;

await searchParams.AppendAsync("page", "2");
var serialized = await searchParams.ToStringAsync();
// tag=one&tag=two&page=2
```

Iterate the live browser collection:

```csharp
await foreach (var entry in searchParams.EntriesAsync())
{
    // entry is a KeyValuePair<string, string>.
}
```

